(13011, 119, 'Ashish Dubey ', 'DM Calculator', 'Development', 'Front-end and Back-end ', 'Overall report kaafi achhi hai, lekin hamari discussion ke kuch important architectural decisions aur implementation details isme missing hain. Main unhe niche merge kar raha hoon. Inhe relevant sections me add kar do.\n\n---\n\n# Additional Work Completed During Today\'s Session\n\n## Notification Architecture Refactoring\n\nThe notification system was redesigned to follow a service-oriented architecture instead of invoking Email and WhatsApp providers directly from business controllers.\n\nA new generic notification layer was introduced under `server/services/notificationService.js`. The objective was to completely decouple business logic from communication channels.\n\nThe new service acts as a centralized dispatcher responsible for orchestrating all outbound notifications. Controllers no longer need to know whether a notification is delivered through Email, WhatsApp, or any future channel.\n\nThe service aggregates delivery results from every provider and returns a unified status object. Notifications are executed independently so that a failure in one channel never blocks the remaining channels.\n\nThis architecture lays the foundation for future integrations such as SMS, Push Notifications, Slack, Microsoft Teams, or any additional communication provider without modifying payment or proposal business logic.\n\n---\n\n# Public Document Security Architecture\n\nA secure public document access system was designed for both Proposals and Final Invoices.\n\nInstead of exposing predictable URLs containing database IDs, every public document is now protected using signed JWT tokens.\n\nEach generated token is hashed using SHA-256 before being stored in the database. The original JWT is never stored, ensuring that database exposure cannot be used to recreate valid public links.\n\nEvery resend operation generates a completely new JWT, replacing the previously stored hash. As a result, previously shared public links become invalid automatically.\n\nThe validation flow performs multiple security checks before exposing any document:\n\n* JWT signature verification\n* Token expiry validation\n* SHA-256 hash verification\n* Document existence validation\n* Document type isolation (`proposal`, `proforma`, `invoice`)\n* Token revocation support\n\nThis implementation significantly improves document security while allowing clients to securely access proposals and invoices without authentication.\n\n---\n\n# Proposal Snapshot Architecture\n\nA proposal snapshot strategy was designed to preserve document consistency after sharing.\n\nWhen a proposal is sent to a client, the exact proposal payload is stored as a snapshot instead of relying on live database records.\n\nBoth the public HTML preview and downloadable PDF are generated from this immutable snapshot.\n\nAs a result, any future edits performed by administrators never modify documents that have already been shared with clients.\n\nThis guarantees that clients always view exactly the same version of the proposal that was originally approved and sent.\n\n---\n\n# Payment Workflow Redesign\n\nThe payment workflow was redesigned around the Proposal ? Payment ? Final Invoice lifecycle.\n\nInstead of manually sending invoices through a dedicated action, invoice generation is now tied directly to successful payment completion.\n\nThe workflow evaluates the remaining outstanding balance using a configurable payment tolerance to safely handle floating-point precision.\n\nIf the proposal is only partially paid, the payment is recorded while invoice generation is skipped.\n\nOnly after the proposal is fully settled does the system:\n\n* Generate the Final Invoice\n* Generate a secure public access token\n* Dispatch notifications\n* Record delivery status\n* Update the payment timeline\n\nThis removes manual intervention while ensuring invoices are generated only after complete payment settlement.\n\n---\n\n# Legacy Workflow Cleanup\n\nA comprehensive cleanup of obsolete business logic was completed.\n\nThe following legacy workflows were removed from the application:\n\n* Quotation notification pipeline\n* Assignment notification pipeline\n* Strategy notification pipeline\n* Assignment reminder CRON workflow\n* Proforma sending workflow\n* Legacy quotation management UI\n* Legacy notification routes\n* Deprecated notification helpers\n* Unused wrappers\n* Obsolete imports and exports\n\nAll remaining notification flows now align exclusively with the new business lifecycle:\n\n**Proposal ? Payment ? Final Invoice**\n\n---\n\n# Notification Reliability Improvements\n\nNotification delivery was redesigned to be completely independent from database transactions.\n\nThe execution sequence now follows this order:\n\nDatabase Transaction\n\n?\n\nPayment Recording\n\n?\n\nProposal Status Update\n\n?\n\nFinal Invoice Generation\n\n?\n\nDatabase COMMIT\n\n?\n\nNotification Dispatch\n\n?\n\nTimeline Logging\n\n?\n\nAPI Response\n\nBecause notifications execute only after a successful commit, failures in Email or WhatsApp delivery can never roll back payment records or invoice generation.\n\nEvery delivery channel reports its own success or failure independently, allowing the frontend to present accurate delivery status to administrators.\n\n---\n\n# Codebase Maintainability Improvements\n\nSeveral architectural improvements were introduced to improve long-term maintainability.\n\nBusiness logic, notification orchestration, public document access, and communication providers are now clearly separated into independent layers.\n\nDead notification code, duplicated logic, obsolete controllers, legacy routes, and redundant helper methods were removed to reduce technical debt.\n\nThe resulting codebase is cleaner, easier to maintain, simpler to extend, and better aligned with the current Revenue Engine workflow.\n', '0', '0', 550, 0, '2026-07-08', '', '', '', 0, '', 0),
