(13052, 119, 'Ashish Dubey ', 'DM Calculator', 'Development', 'Front-end and Back-end ', 'Detailed Daily Task & Engineering Report\nDate: July 11, 2026 Project: Revenue Engine Workspace\n\nThis document serves as an in-depth engineering and task report, detailing the architectural decisions, bug fixes, UI/UX optimizations, and compliance integrations executed during today\'s sprints.\n\n1. Invoice Generation & Legal Compliance Refactoring\nFiles Impacted: AdminInvoice.jsx, PDFGenerator (implied integration), proposalController.js\n\nContext & Problem Statement: The invoicing engine had legacy calculation blocks that caused confusion during partial billings. It displayed a negative, red-colored \"Taxable Value Billed Earlier\" field. Furthermore, the handling of deferred billing amounts and statutory GST disclaimers was lacking.\n\nImplemented Solutions:\n\nBilling History Restructure:\nCompletely removed the confusing \"Taxable Value Billed Earlier\" row.\nReplaced it with a transparent \"Total Base Amount Received\" row. This logic automatically sums all previously received payments against the active proforma, offering clear visibility into paid-up capital without using negative UI indicators.\nSmart Invoice Referencing:\nImplemented historical tracking for invoices. If the current invoice is part of an ongoing billing cycle for a specific proforma, the system automatically detects the immediately preceding invoice and embeds its unique Invoice No (e.g., GST/INV/010) into the document as a reference point.\nDeferred Amount Algorithm:\nRefactored the math behind the \"Less: Amount to be Billed Separately\" section. The system now dynamically calculates the exact difference between the total contract value and the currently billed value, correctly separating deferred GST taxes.\nStatutory & Legal Embeds:\nIntegrated hardcoded legal footnotes to ensure strict compliance with Indian Tax Laws.\nAdded clauses pertaining to Rule 33 of the CGST Rules, 2017 clarifying that third-party media spends (Advertising Budgets) are reimbursements and not subject to service revenue taxation.\nAdded clauses stating that deferred amounts will be billed later and will be subject to GST at the time of future billing as per the CGST Act, 2017.\n2. Service Management Architecture Overhaul\nFiles Impacted: AdminAddServices.jsx, CalculatorBD.jsx, ProposalBuilder.jsx, server/controller.js\n\nContext & Problem Statement: The administration panel required users to navigate a 3-step hierarchical flow (Service ? Category ? Editing Type) to create products. Furthermore, Editing Type was strictly mandatory at the database level, forcing administrators to use placeholder text like \"(Not Applicable)\", which cluttered proposals.\n\nImplemented Solutions:\n\nUI Workflow Consolidation:\nCondensed the 3-step process into an intuitive 2-Step Flow.\nStep 1: Create a Service.\nStep 2: Select the Service, input the Category, define the Amount, and optionally provide an Editing Type.\nDatabase Schema Modification (Critical Fix):\nThe database was actively blocking optional Editing Types by throwing a 500 Internal Server Error (ER_BAD_NULL_ERROR) because the editing_type_name column had a NOT NULL constraint.\nExecuted a raw SQL migration (ALTER TABLE editing_types MODIFY COLUMN editing_type_name varchar(100) NULL) to allow null values natively.\nAPI & Controller Enhancements:\nRefactored the addEditingTypes API endpoint. The backend now securely receives and processes raw null inputs instead of failing or coercing them into strings.\nImplemented a strict Duplicate Protection layer in addCategories to prevent administrators from accidentally creating two categories with the exact same name under the same parent service.\nFrontend Error Mitigation (UX Fix):\nFixed a disruptive \"False-Positive\" bug. When a user created a brand-new service and selected it in the dropdown, the frontend attempted to fetch categories. The backend correctly responded with a 404 Not Found (since no categories existed yet). The frontend previously treated this 404 as a critical failure, throwing a massive Red Swal alert. The catch block was rewritten to silently handle 404s and gracefully return an empty array.\nClean Display Logic:\nUpdated downstream UI components (ProposalBuilder.jsx, AdminCalculator.jsx). Whenever an editing_type_name returns as null from the database, the UI now elegantly hides dependent dropdowns or replaces concatenated strings with a clean - dash, completely eliminating messy (null) text in client-facing documents.\n3. Client Ledger Algorithm Correction\nFiles Impacted: RevenueHistory.jsx\n\nContext & Problem Statement: The \"Client Ledgers\" table inside the Revenue History dashboard was mathematically inaccurate. While the global \"Executive Overview\" correctly displayed pending amounts (e.g., ?14,281), the specific Client Row incorrectly displayed a much lower amount (e.g., ?6,300).\n\nImplemented Solutions:\n\nRoot Cause Analysis:\nTraced the bug to a flawed JavaScript reduce() function operating on the frontend. The legacy code iterated through invoices grouped by client, but it initialized total_pending = Number(inv.current_amt) exactly once. If a client had multiple active proformas, the loop simply ignored the pending balances of all older proformas and only displayed the pending balance of the most recent invoice.\nProforma Aggregation Implementation:\nRe-architected the grouping algorithm.\nIntroduced a seen_proformas mapping mechanism (using new Set()).\nThe loop now tracks every unique proforma_id associated with a specific client. It securely intercepts the latest invoice for every single proforma a client has, and accurately accumulates the current_amt (remaining balance).\nResult:\nThe mathematical integrity of the dashboard is restored. Client Ledger rows now perfectly sum the total outstanding debts across their entire portfolio of active proformas, mirroring the global overview exactly.', '0', '0', 500, 0, '2026-07-11', '', '', '', 0, '', 0),
